var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Getting-Started","page":"Getting Started","text":"A plan holds everything that depends only on the nodes and the array size: the chosen window, the binned nodes and the FFT plans. Creating it costs about as much as a few transforms; afterwards every transform with these nodes is fast.","title":"Getting Started"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#A-first-transform","page":"Getting Started","text":"Nodes are given as a d  J matrix with entries in -12 12); in one dimension a vector works as well. The second argument is the size of the equidistant array.\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nJ, N = 2000, (64, 48)\nk = rand(2, J) .- 0.5\np = plan_nfft(k, N)\nNFFTPlan{Float64} for an input of size (64, 48) along dims 1:2 at 2000 nodes: ExpSemicircle window, W = 10, σ = 1.65, predicted error 9.124339286200806e-9\n\nThe plan behaves like the J  bmN matrix of the NDFT:\n\nsize(p)\n(2000, 3072)\n\nApplying it to an array of size N gives the values at the nodes, the adjoint maps back:\n\nf = randn(ComplexF64, N)\nfHat = p * f\ng = adjoint(p) * fHat\nsize(fHat), size(g)\n((2000,), (64, 48))","title":"A first transform"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#How-accurate-is-it?","page":"Getting Started","text":"Without further options the plan aims at a relative error of 10^-8. accuracy(p) is the error the plan predicts for itself. We compare with the direct evaluation (NDFT), which NFFT.jl offers as a plan of its own:\n\npd = NDFTPlan(k, N)\naccuracy(p), norm(p * f - pd * f) / norm(pd * f)\n(9.124339286200806e-9, 5.52574435345078e-9)\n\nA different tolerance is a keyword away. The plan then chooses a narrower or wider window:\n\np4 = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-4)\np12 = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-12)\n[norm(q * f - pd * f) / norm(pd * f) for q in (p4, p12)]\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 5.522884727394502e-5\n 4.81993225490004e-13","title":"How accurate is it?"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Avoiding-allocations","page":"Getting Started","text":"p * f allocates its output. In loops, use mul! with preallocated arrays:\n\nmul!(fHat, p, f)\nmul!(g, adjoint(p), fHat);","title":"Avoiding allocations"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Single-precision","page":"Getting Started","text":"eltype selects the precision of the data; the default follows the nodes. In single precision the positions of the nodes on the grid are still computed in double precision, so large grids do not lose accuracy.\n\np32 = plan_nfft(k, N; eltype=Float32, reltol=1e-5)\nfHat32 = p32 * ComplexF32.(f)\neltype(fHat32), norm(fHat32 - pd * f) / norm(pd * f)\n(ComplexF32, 2.5873535245051433e-6)","title":"Single precision"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Threads","page":"Getting Started","text":"All stages run multi-threaded with Threads.nthreads() threads (start Julia with julia -t 8 or -t auto). nthreads limits the number for one plan:\n\np1 = plan_nfft(k, N; nthreads=1)\np1 * f ≈ p * f\ntrue\n\nOne plan must not be used by several tasks at the same time, since it owns its workspace. copy(p) gives a plan with its own workspace that shares the read-only parts:\n\nplans = [copy(p) for _ in 1:2]\nresults = Vector{Vector{ComplexF64}}(undef, 2)\nThreads.@threads for i in 1:2\n  results[i] = plans[i] * f\nend\nresults[1] == results[2]\ntrue","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#New-nodes","page":"Getting Started","text":"nodes! exchanges the nodes and keeps all other settings; the number of nodes may change.\n\nnodes!(p, rand(2, 3000) .- 0.5)\nsize(p * f)\n(3000,)","title":"New nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/getting_started/#Without-a-plan","page":"Getting Started","text":"For a single transform, nfft and nfft_adjoint create the plan internally:\n\nfHat = nfft(k, f)\ng = nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat)\nsize(g)\n(64, 48)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Without a plan"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Abstract-Interface","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"AbstractNFFTs.jl defines what an NFFT plan is, independent of how it is computed. NFFT.jl is one implementation; wrappers make FINUFFT and NFFT3 available through the same interface, and NonuniformFFTs.jl implements it as well. Code written against the interface runs with each of them.\n\n(Image: NFFT.jl package family)\n\nnote: Note\nIf you just want to compute NFFTs, use NFFT.jl and ignore this page.","title":"Abstract Interface"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Backends","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"Each implementation has a backend type. Loading NFFT.jl activates its backend; others are chosen explicitly, globally or within a scope:\n\nplan_nfft(k, N)                                  # active backend\nplan_nfft(NFFT.backend(), k, N)                  # explicit backend\nAbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!(NFFT.backend())\nNFFT.activate!()                                 # the same, by convention\nwith(nfft_backend => OtherPackage.backend()) do  # within a scope\n  nfft(k, f)\nend","title":"Backends"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Options:-generic-and-specific","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"Plan constructors take two kinds of keyword arguments:\n\nGeneric options mean the same for every implementation and must be accepted by all of them:\nkeyword meaning\nreltol requested relative error (ell^2 over random data), default 1e-8\ndims dimensions to transform: :, an integer or a contiguous range; the others are batch dimensions\neltype real element type of the data, default from the nodes\nstorage array type of data and workspace (Array, a GPU array type), default from the nodes\nnthreads number of CPU threads, default Threads.nthreads()\nSpecific options belong to one implementation, such as W, σ and window of NFFT.jl. An implementation must reject options it does not know with an ArgumentError, never ignore them silently. Code that uses only generic options is portable.","title":"Options: generic and specific"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Operations","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"function meaning\nmul!(fHat, p, f), p * f NFFT\nmul!(f, adjoint(p), fHat), adjoint(p) * fHat adjoint NFFT (transpose for NFCT/NFST)\nsize_in(p), size_out(p), size(p) array sizes; size(p) is the matrix size\nnodes(p), nodes!(p, k) query and exchange the nodes\naccuracy(p) predicted relative error\nnfft(k, f), nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat), … one-shot functions that create a plan\n\nThe stage interface is optional and meant for implementations that are built from the three classical stages.","title":"Operations"},{"category":"section","location":"abstract/#Writing-an-implementation","page":"Abstract Interface","text":"An implementation defines a backend, a plan type and the operations:\n\nstruct MyBackend <: AbstractNFFTBackend end\nactivate!() = AbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!(MyBackend())\nbackend() = MyBackend()\n\nmutable struct MyPlan{T,D,R} <: AbstractNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n  ...\nend\n\nfunction AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfft(b::MyBackend, k::AbstractMatrix, N::Dims; kwargs...)\n  g, rest = generic_options(k, N; kwargs...)     # reltol, dims, eltype, storage, nthreads\n  check_options(b, rest, (:myoption,))           # reject everything else\n  ...\nend\n\nAbstractNFFTs.size_in(p::MyPlan) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.size_out(p::MyPlan) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.nodes(p::MyPlan) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.nodes!(p::MyPlan, k) = ...\nAbstractNFFTs.accuracy(p::MyPlan) = ...\nLinearAlgebra.mul!(fHat, p::MyPlan, f) = ...\nLinearAlgebra.mul!(f, p::Adjoint{<:Complex,<:MyPlan}, fHat) = ...\n\nIn the type parameters, T is the real element type of the data, D the number of dimensions of the input and R that of the output (R = 1 without batch dimensions). Everything else, *, nfft, size, ChainRules, is derived from these functions.\n\nThe test suite of NFFT.jl contains contract tests that check an implementation against the NDFT for all generic options; they take a plan constructor as an argument and can be run against any backend.","title":"Writing an implementation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#Transforms-along-Dimensions","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"Many applications transform only some dimensions of an array and keep the others: the coils of a multi-coil MRI acquisition, the lateral position in OCT, time frames or channels. With dims the plan transforms along a contiguous range of dimensions; all other dimensions are batch dimensions and pass through unchanged.","title":"Transforms along Dimensions"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#One-dimension-of-a-matrix","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"A 1D NFFT along the first dimension of a 16  20 matrix, with 11 nodes:\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\ny = rand(11) .- 0.5\nf = randn(ComplexF64, 16, 20)\np1 = plan_nfft(y, size(f); dims=1)\nsize(p1 * f)\n(11, 20)\n\nThe nodes replace the transformed dimension. Along the second dimension:\n\np2 = plan_nfft(y, size(f); dims=2)\nsize(p2 * f)\n(16, 11)\n\nThe result equals one NFFT per column (or row):\n\nq = plan_nfft(y, 16)\np1 * f ≈ reduce(hcat, [q * f[:, c] for c in 1:20])\ntrue","title":"One dimension of a matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#Several-dimensions:-multi-coil-MRI","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"Images of L receive coils are stored as an N_1  N_2  L array; each coil is sampled at the same 2D trajectory. A plan with dims = 1:2 transforms all coils at once and returns a J  L array:\n\nL = 8\nN = (128, 128)\nk = rand(2, 20_000) .- 0.5\ncoils = randn(ComplexF64, N..., L)\np = plan_nfft(k, size(coils); dims=1:2)\ndata = p * coils\nsize(data)\n(20000, 8)\n\nThe adjoint maps the J  L data back to the coil images:\n\nsize(adjoint(p) * data)\n(128, 128, 8)","title":"Several dimensions: multi-coil MRI"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/directional/#Why-not-a-loop?","page":"Transforms along Dimensions","text":"The plan processes the slices together. With few slices (here 8 coils) it evaluates the window once per node and applies it to all slices with SIMD instructions; batch dimensions behind dims are first transposed into this layout. Compared with a loop over single plans:\n\nsingle = plan_nfft(k, N)\nout = similar(data)\nloop!(out, single, coils) = for c in 1:L\n  mul!(view(out, :, c), single, view(coils, :, :, c))\nend\nloop!(out, single, coils); mul!(data, p, coils)        # compile\nt_loop = minimum(@elapsed(loop!(out, single, coils)) for _ in 1:5)\nt_batch = minimum(@elapsed(mul!(data, p, coils)) for _ in 1:5)\n(loop_ms=1e3t_loop, batch_ms=1e3t_batch, speedup=t_loop / t_batch)\n(loop_ms = 6.787439, batch_ms = 4.289753999999999, speedup = 1.5822443431488147)\n\nand the results agree:\n\nout ≈ data\ntrue\n\nWith many slices (at least four per thread), or with at least one small slice per thread, each thread transforms whole slices on its own single-threaded copy of the plan, which shares nodes, window and correction factors. Small transforms cannot use many threads, so this is much faster for, e.g., many short 1D transforms:\n\nk1 = rand(1, 2048) .- 0.5\npm = plan_nfft(k1, (2048, 512); dims=1)\nf1 = randn(ComplexF64, 2048, 512); y1 = pm * f1\nsingle1 = plan_nfft(k1, 2048)\nloop1!(y, p, f) = for c in axes(f, 2)\n  mul!(view(y, :, c), p, view(f, :, c))\nend\nloop1!(y1, single1, f1)\n(loop_ms=1e3minimum(@elapsed(loop1!(y1, single1, f1)) for _ in 1:5),\n slices_ms=1e3minimum(@elapsed(mul!(y1, pm, f1)) for _ in 1:5))\n(loop_ms = 89.406972, slices_ms = 14.539229)\n\nBatch kernels are used for 1D and 2D transforms with few slices; 3D transforms with few slices and batch dimensions on both sides of dims loop over the slices.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Why not a loop?"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API","page":"API","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Plans-and-transforms","page":"API","text":"","title":"Plans and transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Windows-and-FFT","page":"API","text":"","title":"Windows and FFT"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Stages","page":"API","text":"","title":"Stages"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#NFFTTools","page":"API","text":"","title":"NFFTTools"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Direct-transforms","page":"API","text":"","title":"Direct transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Abstract-interface","page":"API","text":"","title":"Abstract interface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Index","page":"API","text":"","title":"Index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfft","page":"API","text":"plan_nfft([backend,] k, N; reltol=1e-8, dims=:, eltype, storage, nthreads, kwargs...)\n\nPlan an NFFT for the nodes k (a d × J matrix, or a vector for d = 1, entries in [-1/2, 1/2)) and an equidistant array of size N (an integer or a tuple; with dims, the size of the whole array including batch dimensions). Without backend the active backend is used.\n\nGeneric options, accepted by every implementation:\n\nreltol: requested relative error (ℓ² norm over random data)\ndims: dimensions to transform (:, an integer or a contiguous range); the other dimensions are batch dimensions and pass through unchanged\neltype: real element type of the data, default float(eltype(k))\nstorage: array type of data and workspace, default the array type of k\nnthreads: number of CPU threads, default Threads.nthreads()\n\nFurther keyword arguments are specific to the implementation; an implementation throws an ArgumentError for options it does not know.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nnfft","page":"API","text":"plan_nnfft([backend,] k, y; kwargs...)\n\nPlan an NNFFT with non-equidistant nodes k in the frequency domain and y in the spatial domain. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nnfft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfct","page":"API","text":"plan_nfct([backend,] k, N; kwargs...)\n\nPlan a non-equidistant fast cosine transform (NFCT) for nodes k in [0, 1/2] and a real array of size N. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfct"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfst","page":"API","text":"plan_nfst([backend,] k, N; kwargs...)\n\nPlan a non-equidistant fast sine transform (NFST) for nodes k in [0, 1/2] and a real array of size N. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_nfst"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.plan_rnfft","page":"API","text":"plan_rnfft([backend,] k, N; kwargs...)\n\nPlan an NFFT with real values at the nodes k (in [-1/2, 1/2)) for an array of logical size N (the NFFT counterpart of FFTW's brfft/rfft). The input of the direct transform is the half h of the coefficients with n₁ = 0 … N₁÷2, an array of size (N₁÷2+1, N₂, …) whose other dimensions are indexed as in plan_nfft; the direct transform is\n\nf̂_j = Re Σ_n h_n exp(-2πi n·k_j),\n\nthe adjoint maps real values y at the nodes to h_n = Σ_j y_j exp(2πi n·k_j). The two are adjoint with respect to the real inner product. With dims, the first of the transformed dimensions is the halved one. Keyword arguments as for plan_nfft.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.plan_rnfft"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFFTPlan","page":"API","text":"NFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nNFFT plan of NFFT.jl, created by plan_nfft(k, N; kwargs...) or NFFTPlan(k, N; kwargs...).\n\nGeneric options (every AbstractNFFTs implementation): reltol (default 1e-8), dims, eltype, storage, nthreads. Options of NFFT.jl:\n\nwindow: :auto (default; all windows with a parameter table compete), KaiserBessel(), ExpSemicircle() or a CustomWindow (needs W and σ)\nW: window width (integer, odd allowed; W = 2m in the notation of Knopp et al. 2023)\nσ: oversampling factor\nfft: FFT backend, default FFTWBackend()\ntilesize: expert option, tile edge of the node binning\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFCTPlan","page":"API","text":"NFCTPlan{T,D,R}\nNFSTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nNFCT and NFST plans of NFFT.jl, created by plan_nfct(k, N; kwargs...) and plan_nfst(k, N; kwargs...), with the definitions of NFFT3: for nodes k in [0, 1/2]^d and a real array f of size N,\n\nNFCT: f̂_j = Σ_{n=0}^{N-1} f_n Π_d cos(2π n_d k_dj),   NFST: f̂_j = Σ_{n=1}^{N} f_n Π_d sin(2π n_d k_dj)\n\n(the array index of mode n is n + 1 for the NFCT and n for the NFST). transpose(p) is the transposed transform. Options as for NFFTPlan (without tilesize for batch dimensions; batch dimensions loop over the slices).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFCTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFSTPlan","page":"API","text":"NFCTPlan{T,D,R}\nNFSTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nNFCT and NFST plans of NFFT.jl, created by plan_nfct(k, N; kwargs...) and plan_nfst(k, N; kwargs...), with the definitions of NFFT3: for nodes k in [0, 1/2]^d and a real array f of size N,\n\nNFCT: f̂_j = Σ_{n=0}^{N-1} f_n Π_d cos(2π n_d k_dj),   NFST: f̂_j = Σ_{n=1}^{N} f_n Π_d sin(2π n_d k_dj)\n\n(the array index of mode n is n + 1 for the NFCT and n for the NFST). transpose(p) is the transposed transform. Options as for NFFTPlan (without tilesize for batch dimensions; batch dimensions loop over the slices).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFSTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.RNFFTPlan","page":"API","text":"RNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nRNFFT plan of NFFT.jl (real values at the nodes), created by plan_rnfft(k, N; kwargs...), see plan_rnfft. Options as for NFFTPlan; batch dimensions loop over the slices.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.RNFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NFFTBackend","page":"API","text":"NFFTBackend()\n\nThe AbstractNFFTs backend of NFFT.jl. Activated when the package is loaded; reactivate with NFFT.activate!().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NFFTBackend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFT.parameters","page":"API","text":"NFFT.parameters(p::NFFTPlan) -> (; window, W, σ)\n\nWindow family, window width and oversampling factor the plan uses.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.parameters"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.accuracy","page":"API","text":"accuracy(p) -> Float64\n\nRelative error (ℓ² norm over random data) the plan p is expected to achieve; what the requested reltol turned into.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.accuracy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nodes","page":"API","text":"nodes(p) -> k\n\nNodes of the plan p (a d × J matrix).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nodes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nodes!","page":"API","text":"nodes!(p, k) -> p\n\nChange nodes k in the plan p operation and return the plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nodes!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.size_in","page":"API","text":"size_in(p)\n\nSize of the input array for the plan p (NFFT/NFCT/NFST/NNFFT).  The returned tuple has D entries. Note that this will be the output size for the transposed / adjoint operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.size_in"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.size_out","page":"API","text":"size_out(p)\n\nSize of the output array for the plan p (NFFT/NFCT/NFST/NNFFT).  The returned tuple has R entries.  Note that this will be the input size for the transposed / adjoint operator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.size_out"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LinearAlgebra.mul!","page":"API","text":"mul!(fHat, p, f) -> fHat\n\nInplace NFFT/NFCT/NFST/NNFFT transforming the D dimensional array f to the R dimensional array fHat. The transformation is applied along D-R+1 dimensions specified in the plan p.\n\n\n\n\n\nmul!(f, p, fHat) -> f\n\nInplace adjoint NFFT/NNFFT transforming the R dimensional array fHat to the D dimensional array f. The transformation is applied along D-R+1 dimensions specified in the plan p.\n\n\n\n\n\nmul!(f, p, fHat) -> f\n\nInplace transposed NFCT/NFST transforming the R dimensional array fHat to the D dimensional array f. The transformation is applied along D-R+1 dimensions specified in the plan p.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LinearAlgebra.mul!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#Base.:*","page":"API","text":"    *(p, f) -> fHat\n\nFor a non-directional D dimensional plan p this calculates the NFFT/NNFFT of a D dimensional array f of size N. fHat is a vector of length M. (M and N are defined in the plan creation)\n\nFor a directional D dimensional plan p both f and fHat are D dimensional arrays, and the dimension specified in the plan creation is affected.\n\n\n\n\n\n    *(p, f) -> fHat\n\nFor a non-directional D dimensional plan p this calculates the NFCT/NFST of a D dimensional array f of size N. fHat is a vector of length M. (M and N are defined in the plan creation)\n\nFor a directional D dimensional plan p both f and fHat are D dimensional arrays, and the dimension specified in the plan creation is affected.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.:*"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfft","page":"API","text":"nfft(k, f, rest...; kwargs...)\nnfft(backend, k, f, rest...; kwargs...)\n\ncalculates the nfft of the array f for the nodes contained in the matrix k The output is a vector of length M=size(nodes,2).\n\nUses the active AbstractNFFTs backend if no backend argument is provided. Backends can be activated with BackendModule.activate!(). Backends can also be set with a scoped value overriding the current active backend within a scope:\n\njulia> NFFT.activate!()\n\njulia> nfft(k, f, rest...; kwargs...) # uses NFFT\n\njulia> with(nfft_backend => NonuniformFFTs.backend()) do\n          nfft(k, f, rest...; kwargs...) # uses NonuniformFFTs\n       end\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfft"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfft_adjoint","page":"API","text":"nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...)\nnfft_adjoint(backend, k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...)\n\ncalculates the adjoint nfft of the vector fHat for the nodes contained in the matrix k. The output is an array of size N.\n\nUses the active AbstractNFFTs backend if no backend argument is provided. Backends can be activated with BackendModule.activate!(). Backends can also be set with a scoped value overriding the current active backend within a scope:\n\njulia> NFFT.activate!()\n\njulia> nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...) # uses NFFT\n\njulia> with(nfft_backend => NonuniformFFTs.backend()) do\n          nfft_adjoint(k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...) # uses NonuniformFFTs\n       end\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfft_adjoint"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfct","page":"API","text":"nfct(k, f, rest...; kwargs...)\nnfct(backend, k, f, rest...; kwargs...)\n\ncalculates the nfct of the array f for the nodes contained in the matrix k The output is a vector of length M=size(nodes,2).\n\nUses the active AbstractNFFTs backend if no backend argument is provided. Backends can be activated with BackendModule.activate!().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfct"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfct_transpose","page":"API","text":"nfct_transpose(k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...)\nnfct_transpose(backend, k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...)\n\ncalculates the transpose nfct of the vector fHat for the nodes contained in the matrix k. The output is an array of size N.\n\nUses the active AbstractNFFTs backend if no backend argument is provided. Backends can be activated with BackendModule.activate!().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfct_transpose"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfst","page":"API","text":"nfst(k, f, rest...; kwargs...)\nnfst(backend, k, f, rest...; kwargs...)\n\ncalculates the nfst of the array f for the nodes contained in the matrix k The output is a vector of length M=size(nodes,2).\n\nUses the active AbstractNFFTs backend if no backend argument is provided. Backends can be activated with BackendModule.activate!().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfst"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.nfst_transpose","page":"API","text":"nfst_transpose(k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...)\nnfst_transpose(backend, k, N, fHat, rest...; kwargs...)\n\ncalculates the transpose nfst of the vector fHat for the nodes contained in the matrix k. The output is an array of size N.\n\nUses the active AbstractNFFTs backend if no backend argument is provided. Backends can be activated with BackendModule.activate!().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.nfst_transpose"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.AbstractWindow","page":"API","text":"AbstractWindow\n\nWindow function of the NFFT. A window is evaluated in grid units, φ(z) for z ∈ [-W/2, W/2] (W the window width, W = 2m in the notation of Knopp et al. 2023); it is symmetric and zero outside. The resampling evaluates a piecewise polynomial fitted to φ, so the cost of φ itself does not matter.\n\nFamilies with a parameter table (used by the automatic parameter choice): KaiserBessel, ExpSemicircle. Any other window can be used with explicit W and σ through CustomWindow.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.AbstractWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.KaiserBessel","page":"API","text":"KaiserBessel()\n\nContinuous Kaiser–Bessel window (Potts & Tasche 2021): the sinh form sinh(β√(1−(2z/W)²))/√(1−(2z/W)²) minus its edge value β, so that it vanishes at |z| = W/2. The shape parameter β is taken from the kernel table (optimised per W and σ).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.KaiserBessel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.ExpSemicircle","page":"API","text":"ExpSemicircle()\n\nExponential of semicircle family, φ(z) = exp(β((1−(2z/W)²)^e₀ − 1)). e₀ = 1/2 is the ES kernel of FINUFFT (Barnett et al. 2019); a free exponent e₀ is the family used by ducc (Arras et al. 2021). β and e₀ are taken from the kernel table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.ExpSemicircle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.CustomWindow","page":"API","text":"CustomWindow(φ)\n\nUser-defined window: φ(z, W) must be symmetric in z and zero for |z| ≥ W/2. Requires explicit W and σ in the plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.CustomWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.AbstractFFTBackend","page":"API","text":"AbstractFFTBackend\n\nFFT used for the oversampled grid. A backend must provide in-place batched 1D transforms along a chosen dimension of an array view (forward and unnormalised backward).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.AbstractFFTBackend"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.FFTWBackend","page":"API","text":"FFTWBackend(; flags = FFTW.ESTIMATE)\n\nFFTW on the CPU. In D ≥ 2 NFFT.jl threads the per-dimension passes itself over single-threaded FFTW plans: FFTW's own threading with ESTIMATE produces plans up to 17× slower than MEASURE, while single-threaded batched ESTIMATE plans are within 10–20 % of MEASURE. A transform with one dimension uses FFTW's own threading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.FFTWBackend"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_size","page":"API","text":"oversampled_size(p) -> NTuple\n\nSize of the oversampled equidistant array the FFT of plan p acts on (batch dimensions of a plan with dims included).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_size"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction!","page":"API","text":"resampling_correction!(p, f, g) -> g\n\nFirst step of the direct NFFT: divide f by the Fourier transform of the window function and place it into the zero-padded oversampled array g.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_fft!","page":"API","text":"oversampled_fft!(p, g) -> g\n\nSecond step of the direct NFFT: the FFT on the oversampled array g, in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_fft!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling!","page":"API","text":"resampling!(p, g, fHat) -> fHat\n\nThird step of the direct NFFT: resample the oversampled equidistant array g at the nodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling_adjoint!","page":"API","text":"resampling_adjoint!(p, fHat, g) -> g\n\nFirst step of the adjoint NFFT: the adjoint resampling of the values fHat at the nodes onto the oversampled equidistant array g (overwritten).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling_adjoint!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_bfft!","page":"API","text":"oversampled_bfft!(p, g) -> g\n\nSecond step of the adjoint NFFT: the adjoint (unnormalised backward) FFT on g, in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.oversampled_bfft!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction_adjoint!","page":"API","text":"resampling_correction_adjoint!(p, g, f) -> f\n\nThird step of the adjoint NFFT: crop the oversampled array g and divide by the Fourier transform of the window function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.resampling_correction_adjoint!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.sdc","page":"API","text":"weights = sdc(p::AbstractNFFTPlan; iters = 20)\n\nSampling density compensation weights for the nodes of the NFFT plan p, computed with iters iterations of the method of Pipe and Menon (Magn Reson Med 41(1):179–186, 1999, doi:10.1002/(SICI)1522-2594(199901)41:1<179::AID-MRM25>3.0.CO;2-V). The iteration uses the resampling stage of the plan, i.e. the window function on the oversampled grid, as its kernel. The weights therefore depend on the window of the plan rather than on its accuracy: narrow windows give better weights than wide ones. With NFFT.jl, plan_nfft(k, N; W = 4, σ = 2.0) works well (MRI test data: 0.11 relative deviation of a gridding reconstruction from the scanner's, 0.21 with the window chosen for reltol = 1e-3).\n\nThe weights are real, positive and scaled such that A^mathsfH operatornamediag(w) A bm1  bm1. For nodes on the equidistant grid of an array of size N they are ≈ 1/prod(N).\n\np must be a plan without batch dimensions that implements the stage interface of AbstractNFFTs (oversampled_size, resampling!, resampling_adjoint!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.sdc"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.sdc!","page":"API","text":"sdc!(weights, p::AbstractNFFTPlan; iters = 20, workg, workf, workv)\n\nIn-place version of sdc. The work arrays (complex, of sizes oversampled_size(p), size_out(p) and size_in(p)) can be passed to avoid allocations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.sdc!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel","page":"API","text":"calculateToeplitzKernel(shape, tr::AbstractMatrix{T}; fftplan = plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE), kwargs...)\n\nCalculate the kernel for an implementation of the Gram matrix A^mathsfH A that utilizes its Toeplitz structure. The output is an array of twice the size of shape, as the Toeplitz trick requires an oversampling factor of 2 (cf. Wajer, F. T. A. W., and K. P. Pruessmann. \"Major speedup of reconstruction for sensitivity encoding with arbitrary trajectories.\" Proc. Intl. Soc. Mag. Res. Med. 2001.). The type of the kernel is Complex{T}, i.e. the complex of the k-space trajectory's type; for speed and memory efficiency, call this function with Float32.(tr), and the kernel will also be Float32.\n\nThe kernel is computed with an adjoint NFFT of the active AbstractNFFTs backend (e.g. NFFT.jl after using NFFT).\n\nRequired Arguments\n\nshape::NTuple(Int): size of the image; e.g. (256, 256) for 2D imaging, or (256,256,128) for 3D imaging\ntr::Matrix{T}: non-Cartesian k-space trajectory in units revolutions/voxel, i.e. tr[i] ∈ [-0.5, 0.5] ∀ i. The matrix has the size 2 x Nsamples for 2D imaging with a trajectory length Nsamples, and 3 x Nsamples for 3D imaging.\n\nKeyword Arguments:\n\nfftplan: plan for the final FFT of the kernel from image to k-space. Therefore, it has to have twice the size of the original image. default = plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE). If this constructor is used many times, it is worth to precompute the plan with plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.MEASURE) and reuse it.\nkwargs: passed on to plan_nfft, e.g. reltol for the accuracy of the kernel.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using NFFT, NFFTTools\n\njulia> Nx = 32;\n\njulia> trj = Float32.(rand(2, 1000) .- 0.5);\n\njulia> λ = calculateToeplitzKernel((Nx, Nx), trj);\n\njulia> y = randn(ComplexF32, Nx, Nx);\n\njulia> convolveToeplitzKernel!(y, λ);\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel!","page":"API","text":"calculateToeplitzKernel!(f::Array{Complex{T}}, p::AbstractNFFTPlan, tr::Matrix{T}, fftplan)\n\nCalculate the kernel for an implementation of the Gram matrix that utilizes its Toeplitz structure and writes it in-place in f, which has to be twice the size of the desired image matrix, as the Toeplitz trick requires an oversampling factor of 2 (cf. Wajer, F. T. A. W., and K. P. Pruessmann. \"Major speedup of reconstruction for sensitivity encoding with arbitrary trajectories.\" Proc. Intl. Soc. Mag. Res. Med. 2001.). The type of the kernel f has to be Complex{T}, i.e. the complex of the k-space trajectory's type; for speed and memory efficiecy, call this function with Float32.(tr), and set the type of f accordingly.\n\nRequired Arguments\n\nf::Array{T}: Array in which the kernel will be written.\np::AbstractNFFTPlan: NFFT plan for an array of twice the image size; its nodes are set to tr.\ntr::Matrix{T}: non-Cartesian k-space trajectory in units revolutions/voxel, i.e. tr[i] ∈ [-0.5, 0.5] ∀ i. The matrix has the size 2 x Nsamples for 2D imaging with a trajectory length Nsamples, and 3 x Nsamples for 3D imaging.\nfftplan: plan for the final FFT of the kernel from image to k-space. Therefore, it has to have twice the size of the original image. Calculate, e.g., with fftplan = plan_fft(zeros(Complex{T}, 2 .* shape); flags=FFTW.MEASURE), where shape is the size of the reconstructed image.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using NFFT, NFFTTools, NFFTTools.FFTW\n\njulia> Nx = 32;\n\njulia> trj = Float32.(rand(2, 1000) .- 0.5);\n\njulia> p = plan_nfft(trj, (2Nx, 2Nx));\n\njulia> fftplan = plan_fft(zeros(ComplexF32, (2Nx,2Nx)); flags=FFTW.MEASURE);\n\njulia> λ = Array{ComplexF32}(undef, 2Nx, 2Nx);\n\njulia> calculateToeplitzKernel!(λ, p, trj, fftplan);\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.calculateToeplitzKernel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#NFFTTools.convolveToeplitzKernel!","page":"API","text":"convolveToeplitzKernel!(y::Array{T,N}, λ::Array{T,N}[, fftplan = plan_fft(λ; flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE), ifftplan = plan_ifft(λ; flags=FFTW.ESTIMATE), xOS1 = similar(λ), xOS2 = similar(λ)])\n\nConvolves the image y with the Toeplitz kernel λ and overwrites y with the result. y is also returned for convenience. As this function is commonly applied many times, it is highly recommended to pre-allocate / pre-compute all optional arguments. By doing so, this entire function is non-allocating.\n\nRequired Arguments\n\ny::Array{T,N}: Input image that will be overwritten with the result. y is a matrix (N=2) for 2D imaging and a 3D tensor (N=3) for 3D imaging. The type of the elements T must match the ones of λ.\nλ::Array{T,N}: Toeplitz kernel, which as to be the same type as k, but twice the size due to the required oversampling (cf. calculateToeplitzKernel).\n\nOptional, but highly recommended Arguments\n\nfftplan: plan for the oversampled FFT, i.e. it has to have twice the size of the original image. Calculate, e.g., with fftplan = plan_fft(λ; flags=FFTW.MEASURE), where shape is the size of the reconstructed image.\nifftplan: plan for the oversampled inverse FFT. Calculate, e.g., with ifftplan = plan_ifft(λ; flags=FFTW.MEASURE), where shape is the size of the reconstructed image.\nxOS1: pre-allocated array of the size of λ. Pre-allocate with xOS1 = similar(λ).\nxOS2: pre-allocated array of the size of λ. Pre-allocate with xOS2 = similar(λ).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> using NFFT, NFFTTools, NFFTTools.FFTW\n\njulia> Nx = 32;\n\njulia> trj = Float32.(rand(2, 1000) .- 0.5);\n\njulia> λ = calculateToeplitzKernel((Nx, Nx), trj);\n\njulia> xOS1 = similar(λ);\n\njulia> xOS2 = similar(λ);\n\njulia> fftplan = plan_fft(xOS1; flags=FFTW.MEASURE);\n\njulia> ifftplan = plan_ifft(xOS1; flags=FFTW.MEASURE);\n\njulia> y = randn(ComplexF32, Nx, Nx);\n\njulia> convolveToeplitzKernel!(y, λ, fftplan, ifftplan, xOS1, xOS2);\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFTTools.convolveToeplitzKernel!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NDFTPlan","page":"API","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NDFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NDCTPlan","page":"API","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NDCTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NDSTPlan","page":"API","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NDSTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#NFFT.NNDFTPlan","page":"API","text":"NDFTPlan(k, N)\nNDCTPlan(k, N)\nNDSTPlan(k, N)\nNNDFTPlan(k, y)\n\nDirect evaluation of the NDFT, NDCT, NDST and NNDFT with the definitions of the fast plans (O(J·prod(N)) operations), as a reference for tests and small problems.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"NFFT.NNDFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractFTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for any NFFT-like plan (NFFT, NFFT, NFCT, NFST).\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractComplexFTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractComplexFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for either an NFFT plan or an NNFFT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractComplexFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRealFTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractRealFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for either an NFCT plan or an NFST plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRealFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFFTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFFT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNNFFTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractNNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NNFFT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNNFFTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFCTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractNFCTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFCT plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFCTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFSTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractNFSTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFST plan.\n\nT is the element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractNFSTPlan"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRNFFTPlan","page":"API","text":"AbstractRNFFTPlan{T,D,R}\n\nAbstract type for an NFFT plan with real values at the nodes (the NFFT counterpart of FFTW's brfft/rfft): the direct transform maps the half n₁ ≥ 0 of the complex coefficients to real values at the nodes, the adjoint maps real values at the nodes to that half.\n\nT is the real element type (Float32/Float64)\nD is the number of dimensions of the input array.\nR is the number of dimensions of the output array.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.AbstractRNFFTPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!","page":"API","text":"set_active_backend!(back::Union{Missing, Module, AbstractNFFTBackend})\n\nSet the default NFFT plan backend. A module back must implement back.backend().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.set_active_backend!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.DEFAULT_RELTOL","page":"API","text":"DEFAULT_RELTOL\n\nRelative error requested from a plan when reltol is not given.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.DEFAULT_RELTOL"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.GenericOptions","page":"API","text":"GenericOptions\n\nThe options every implementation of the interface accepts, resolved from the keyword arguments of plan_nfft and friends by generic_options:\n\nreltol: requested relative error (ℓ² norm over random data)\ndims: dimensions of the transform (a contiguous range); all other dimensions are batch dimensions that pass through unchanged\neltype: real element type of the data (Float32, Float64, …)\nstorage: array type of the data and the workspace (Array, a GPU array type, …)\nnthreads: number of CPU threads\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.GenericOptions"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.generic_options","page":"API","text":"generic_options(k, N; reltol, dims, eltype, storage, nthreads) -> (GenericOptions, rest)\n\nResolve the generic keyword arguments of a plan constructor for nodes k and array size N, and return the remaining (implementation-specific) keyword arguments untouched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.generic_options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#AbstractNFFTs.check_options","page":"API","text":"check_options(backend, kwargs, supported)\n\nThrow an ArgumentError if kwargs contains a keyword that is not in supported. Every implementation must reject options it does not understand instead of ignoring them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"AbstractNFFTs.check_options"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Performance","page":"Performance","text":"NFFT.jl is compared with FINUFFT, ducc, NonuniformFFTs.jl and its previous version 0.14. All numbers on this page come from the benchmark suite in benchmark/ of the repository and can be reproduced with it (see Reproducing).\n\nSetup of the figures: Apple M1 Max, 8 threads (performance cores), Julia 1.13; FINUFFT and ducc built from their main branches with native CPU flags; FFTW plans with ESTIMATE. Unless stated otherwise J = bmN = 2^18 uniformly random nodes, bmN = 2^18, 512^2, 64^3, double precision. NonuniformFFTs.jl has no tolerance parameter; its kernel width is derived from the tolerance as in NFFT.jl 0.14.","title":"Performance"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Time-against-accuracy","page":"Performance","text":"The libraries interpret their tolerance parameters differently, so they are compared at equal measured error. Each point is one tolerance of a sweep from 10^-2 to 10^-14 (single precision: 10^-6); further right means more accurate, lower means faster.\n\n(Image: Time against accuracy, double precision)\n\n1D: NFFT.jl is the fastest library at every accuracy, 1.4–2× ahead of FINUFFT and ducc.\n2D: NFFT.jl and ducc are within 10 % of each other (NFFT.jl ahead at low accuracy), FINUFFT is 1.2–1.6× slower.\n3D: NFFT.jl and ducc are on par; FINUFFT is up to 4.5× slower for the direct transform.\n\n(Image: Time against accuracy, single precision)\n\nIn single precision NFFT.jl is 1.2–1.8× faster than ducc in every dimension. FINUFFT cannot reach small errors in single precision for these sizes (its tolerance floor grows with N; in 1D it rejects even 10^-2).","title":"Time against accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Threads","page":"Performance","text":"Speedup over one thread at a tolerance of 10^-8 (solid: direct, dashed: adjoint; dotted: ideal).\n\n(Image: Strong scaling)\n\nWith 8 threads NFFT.jl reaches a speedup of 4.4–4.7 in 1D, 5.7–6.2 in 2D and 6.3 in 3D; ducc and FINUFFT scale similarly in 2D and 3D and less in 1D (3.2–3.6).","title":"Threads"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Problem-size","page":"Performance","text":"Time per node for J = bmN uniformly random nodes at a tolerance of 10^-8.\n\n(Image: Problem size)\n\nFor large problems the time per node is 7–10 ns in 1D and 2D and about 30 ns (direct) and 55 ns (adjoint) in 3D. Below about 10^4 nodes the fixed costs of the threaded stages dominate; there NFFT.jl is slower than ducc in 2D and 3D.","title":"Problem size"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Node-distributions","page":"Performance","text":"Uniformly random nodes, radial spokes through the centre (as in MRI) and nodes clustered in the centre (normal distribution), tolerance 10^-8. Solid bars: direct transform, light bars: adjoint.\n\n(Image: Node distributions)\n\nThe distribution of the nodes hardly matters for any of the libraries.","title":"Node distributions"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Many-transforms-with-the-same-nodes","page":"Performance","text":"Time per transform for L transforms with the same nodes (dims, FINUFFT's ntrans; ducc loops over the transforms), tolerance 10^-8.\n\n(Image: Batches)\n\nTransforms with the same nodes share the window evaluation. For many short 1D transforms (OCT: 512 A-scans) NFFT.jl runs the transforms in parallel on single-threaded copies of the plan and needs 7 µs per transform, 13–15× less than FINUFFT and ducc. For multi-coil MRI it is 1.1–2× faster than the others, for volumes on par with ducc.","title":"Many transforms with the same nodes"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Real-data","page":"Performance","text":"The RNFFT (real values at the nodes), the NFCT and the NFST against the complex NFFT of the same size, tolerance 10^-8.\n\n(Image: Real data)\n\nThe RNFFT computes on a real grid and needs about 60 % of the time of the complex NFFT. NFCT and NFST cost about as much as the complex NFFT in 2D and 3D; in 1D they are still slower.","title":"Real data"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#NFFT.jl-1.0-against-0.14","page":"Performance","text":"(Image: Speedup over NFFT.jl 0.14)\n\nSpeedup of NFFT.jl 1.0 over 0.14 at the same requested tolerance, with 1 thread (dashed) and 8 threads (solid). NFFT.jl 0.14 misses its tolerance by a factor of 3–4 (e.g. 3510^-4 for 10^-4), so at equal error the speedups are larger. The large factors in 2D with 8 threads come from FFTW's slow threaded ESTIMATE plans that 0.14 used.","title":"NFFT.jl 1.0 against 0.14"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Explorer","page":"Performance","text":"All measurements of this page. Choose a suite, the quantity on the y axis and the parameters; hovering a point shows the details (including the window width W and oversampling factor σ NFFT.jl chose).\n\n<div id=\"nfft-explorer\"></div>","title":"Explorer"},{"category":"section","location":"performance/#Reproducing","page":"Performance","text":"julia benchmark/bench.jl run fast                  # ≈ 15 s, NFFT.jl alone\njulia benchmark/bench.jl run quick                 # ≈ 1 min, NFFT.jl, FINUFFT and ducc\njulia benchmark/bench.jl run all --save            # all suites of this page, ≈ 15 min\njulia benchmark/bench.jl report                    # figures and explorer data\njulia benchmark/bench.jl ab v1.0.0                 # the working tree against a release\n\nEach library runs in its own process and environment, with its default parameters apart from the tolerance and the number of threads. The error is the relative ell^2 error against the exact NDFT on 64 sampled outputs, the time the minimum over repeated calls of a precomputed plan. Details in benchmark/README.md.","title":"Reproducing"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/rnfft/#Real-Data","page":"Real Data","text":"When the values at the nodes are real, the coefficients are Hermitian, f_-bmn = overlinef_bmn, and half of them suffice. plan_rnfft is the NFFT counterpart of FFTW's brfft/rfft: for an array of logical size bmN its input is the half bmh with n_1 = 0 dots lfloor N_12 rfloor (the other dimensions indexed as in plan_nfft), and\n\nhatf_j = operatornameRe sum_bmn h_bmn  mathrme^-2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j\nqquad\nh_bmn = sum_j=1^J y_j  mathrme^2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j quad text(adjoint)\n\nThe two are adjoint with respect to the real inner product, so they can be used in iterative solvers. The grid of the algorithm is real, which saves half of the FFT and of the adjoint resampling.","title":"Real Data"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/rnfft/#Optical-coherence-tomography","page":"Real Data","text":"In spectral-domain OCT, each A-scan is a real interferogram sampled at wavenumbers that are not equidistant; the depth profile is its Fourier transform. With dims = 1 one plan reconstructs all A-scans at once. A synthetic B-scan with two reflecting layers:\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nJ, Nz, L = 2048, 1024, 256                      # samples per A-scan, depth, A-scans\nk = reshape(sort(0.45 .* sin.(range(-π / 2, π / 2; length=J))), 1, J)   # non-linear in k\ndepth(l) = (180 + round(Int, 20sin(2π * l / L)), 420 + round(Int, 30cos(2π * l / L)))\ny = zeros(J, L)\nfor l in 1:L, (z, a) in zip(depth(l), (1.0, 0.6))\n  y[:, l] .+= a .* cospi.(2z .* k[1, :])\nend\ny .+= 0.2 .* randn(J, L)\n2048×256 Matrix{Float64}:\n  0.412387   -1.64756  -1.85114  …  -1.34529   0.250488   0.671186\n  0.455682   -1.71167  -1.79482     -1.43751   0.301409   0.367789\n  0.280843   -1.4352   -1.26304     -1.2421    0.456857   0.605467\n  0.409372   -1.37064  -1.42736     -1.33699   0.0826031  0.294426\n  0.617285   -1.13178  -1.81106     -1.4508   -0.0616679  0.496799\n  0.0849957  -1.34479  -1.77687  …  -1.55691   0.653185   0.621613\n  0.435828   -1.64793  -1.65832     -1.49115   0.652055   0.243922\n  0.574261   -1.31171  -1.64193     -1.4526    0.298925   0.496229\n -0.156035   -1.47101  -1.39264     -1.85745   0.35623    0.295452\n  0.0248325  -1.80818  -1.7511      -1.17618   0.113407   0.30618\n  ⋮                              ⋱                        ⋮\n  0.679569   -1.86003  -1.6504      -1.41223   0.522395   0.294318\n  0.486614   -1.54224  -1.46935  …  -1.6947    0.153853   0.469989\n  0.461282   -1.82101  -1.39765     -1.55561   0.457367   0.239671\n  0.493735   -1.34551  -1.67179     -1.54374   0.561056   0.44087\n  0.638923   -1.68852  -1.52832     -1.34948   0.478463   0.393733\n  0.448934   -1.40968  -1.50986     -1.55543   0.864329   0.494264\n  0.533523   -1.26084  -1.50699  …  -1.8455    0.458042   0.68596\n  0.466258   -1.83753  -1.3666      -1.5649    0.576231   0.395772\n  0.504592   -1.63918  -1.32497     -1.32456   0.519996   0.0372952\n\nThe depth profiles are the adjoint applied to the interferograms, weighted with the spacing of the wavenumbers (a simple density compensation):\n\np = plan_rnfft(k, (2Nz, L); dims=1)\nw = [k[2] - k[1]; (k[3:end] .- k[1:end-2]) ./ 2; k[end] - k[end-1]]\nh = adjoint(p) * (w .* y)                       # depth profiles, n = 0 … Nz\nsize(h)\n(1025, 256)\n\ndB = 20log10.(abs.(h) ./ maximum(abs, h))\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 360))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"A-scan\", ylabel=\"depth\", yreversed=true)\nhm = heatmap!(ax, 1:L, 0:Nz, dB'; colormap=:grays, colorrange=(-40, 0))\nColorbar(fig[1, 2], hm; label=\"dB\")\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe plan runs the 256 A-scans in parallel (one single-threaded transform per thread):\n\nt = minimum(@elapsed(mul!(h, adjoint(p), w .* y)) for _ in 1:5)\n(ms=1e3t, threads=Threads.nthreads())\n(ms = 3.824399, threads = 4)","title":"Optical coherence tomography"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/rnfft/#Accuracy","page":"Real Data","text":"Against the direct evaluation of the definition:\n\nN = (64, 48)\nkr = rand(2, 500) .- 0.5\npr = plan_rnfft(kr, N)\nhr = randn(ComplexF64, size_in(pr))\nmodes(I) = (I[1] - 1, I[2] - 1 - N[2] ÷ 2)\nref = [sum(real(hr[I] * cispi(-2 * sum(modes(I) .* Tuple(kr[:, j])))) for I in CartesianIndices(hr)) for j in 1:500]\nnorm(pr * hr - ref) / norm(ref), accuracy(pr)\n(3.2980744272001504e-9, 5.747818323944175e-9)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Accuracy"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/tools/#Density-Compensation-and-Toeplitz-Kernels","page":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz","text":"NFFTTools.jl builds on the interface of AbstractNFFTs.jl and works with any implementation that provides the stage interface. It offers sampling density compensation and the Toeplitz kernel of the normal operator bmA^mathsfHbmA.","title":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz Kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/tools/#Sampling-density-compensation","page":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz","text":"The adjoint NFFT is not the inverse of the NFFT. For nodes of non-uniform density, a weighted adjoint bmA^mathsfH operatornamediag(bmw) is a much better approximate inverse. sdc computes weights bmw with the iteration of Pipe and Menon. Here for a radial trajectory, which samples the centre of k-space much more densely than the edge:\n\nusing NFFT, NFFTTools, LinearAlgebra, CairoMakie\n\nN = (64, 64)\nnr, na = 64, 100\nr = range(-0.5, 0.5; length=nr + 1)[1:end-1]\nθ = range(0, π; length=na + 1)[1:end-1]\nk = reduce(hcat, [[ρ * cos(t); ρ * sin(t)] for ρ in r, t in θ]);\n\nThe weights depend on the window of the plan, not on its accuracy; a narrow window gives the best weights:\n\np = plan_nfft(k, N; W=4, σ=2.0)\nw = sdc(p; iters=20)\n\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 320))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"radius\", ylabel=\"weight\")\nlines!(ax, r, reshape(w, nr, na)[:, 1])\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe weights grow linearly with the radius, as the analytical density compensation of a radial trajectory. A reconstruction of a phantom with the weighted adjoint:\n\nphantom = [hypot(x - 32.5, y - 32.5) < 24 ? 1.0 + (hypot(x - 26, y - 40) < 6) : 0.0 for x in 1:64, y in 1:64]\ndata = p * complex.(phantom)\nrecon = adjoint(p) * (w .* data)\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 320))\nheatmap(fig[1, 1], phantom; colormap=:grays, axis=(aspect=1, title=\"phantom\"))\nheatmap(fig[1, 2], real.(recon); colormap=:grays, axis=(aspect=1, title=\"weighted adjoint\"))\nfig\nFigure()","title":"Sampling density compensation"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/tools/#Toeplitz-kernel","page":"Density Compensation and Toeplitz","text":"The normal operator bmA^mathsfHbmA of an NFFT is a Toeplitz matrix, so it can be applied with two FFTs of twice the image size instead of an NFFT and its adjoint. calculateToeplitzKernel computes the kernel once (keyword arguments are passed to plan_nfft), convolveToeplitzKernel! applies it in place:\n\nx = randn(ComplexF64, N)\nλ = calculateToeplitzKernel(N, k; reltol=1e-10)\ny = convolveToeplitzKernel!(copy(x), λ)\npt = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-10)\nnorm(y - adjoint(pt) * (pt * x)) / norm(y)\n5.5449113872077525e-11\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Toeplitz kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/real/#Cosine-and-Sine-Transforms","page":"Cosine and Sine Transforms","text":"For real data with even or odd symmetry the non-equidistant fast cosine and sine transforms (NFCT, NFST) replace the complex NFFT. NFFT.jl uses the definitions of NFFT3: for a real array bmf of size bmN and nodes bmk_j in 0 12^D,\n\ntextNFCTquad hatf_j = sum_bmn=bm0^bmN-bm1 f_bmn prod_d=1^D cos(2pi n_d k_dj)\nqquad\ntextNFSTquad hatf_j = sum_bmn=bm1^bmN f_bmn prod_d=1^D sin(2pi n_d k_dj)\n\nThe array index of mode n_d is n_d + 1 for the NFCT and n_d for the NFST. Instead of an adjoint, the real transforms have a transpose.\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nk = rand(2, 1000) ./ 2            # nodes in [0, 1/2]²\nf = randn(64, 48)\n\np = plan_nfct(k, size(f))\nfHat = p * f\ng = transpose(p) * fHat\nsize(fHat), size(g)\n((1000,), (64, 48))\n\nThe plans take the same options as the NFFT (reltol, dims, eltype, nthreads, window, W, σ) and are checked here against the direct evaluation:\n\nrelerr(a, b) = norm(a - b) / norm(b)\nrelerr(fHat, NDCTPlan(k, size(f)) * f), accuracy(p)\n(3.534271968819994e-9, 5.936924253460223e-9)\n\nq = plan_nfst(k, size(f); reltol=1e-12)\nrelerr(q * f, NDSTPlan(k, size(f)) * f), accuracy(q)\n(4.3786800618710705e-13, 9.79728031815724e-13)","title":"Cosine and Sine Transforms"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/real/#How-it-works","page":"Cosine and Sine Transforms","text":"The NFCT is an NFFT of the even extension of bmf (size 2bmN), the NFST one of the odd extension. Its oversampled grid of 2tildebmN points is even (odd) about 0 and tildebmN, so only the points 0 dots tildeN_d are stored:\n\nResampling correction: divide by hatvarphi as in the NFFT.\nFFT: a DCT-I (DST-I) along each dimension, computed as a real FFT of the extended line.\nResampling: the halo of the grid is filled with mirror images (with a sign change for the NFST), so the resampling loops are the ones of the NFFT, on a real grid.\n\nThe cost is about that of a complex NFFT of size bmN:\n\ntmin(f) = (f(); minimum(@elapsed(f()) for _ in 1:5))\nkc = rand(2, 2^18) ./ 2; kf = rand(2, 2^18) .- 0.5\nfr = randn(256, 256); fc = complex.(fr)\npc = plan_nfct(kc, (256, 256)); pf = plan_nfft(kf, (256, 256))\nyr = zeros(2^18); yc = zeros(ComplexF64, 2^18)\n(NFCT_ms = 1e3 * tmin(() -> mul!(yr, pc, fr)), NFFT_ms = 1e3 * tmin(() -> mul!(yc, pf, fc)))\n(NFCT_ms = 6.811114, NFFT_ms = 6.25787)\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"How it works"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Mathematical-Background","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"This page defines the transforms that NFFT.jl computes and the three stages of the algorithm. The derivation and the error analysis are in the NFFT.jl paper, whose notation we follow.\n\nnote: Note\nThe NFFT is also called NUFFT, and in MRI gridding. FINUFFT and others classify the transforms into types: type 2 is the NFFT, type 1 its adjoint, type 3 the NNFFT with non-equidistant points on both sides.","title":"Mathematical Background"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#NDFT","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"Let bmN in mathbbN^D be the size of a D-dimensional array bmf = (f_bmn)_bmn in I_bmN sampled on a grid, indexed by the centred multi-index set\n\nI_bmN = mathbbZ^D cap prod_d=1^D left-fracN_d2fracN_d2right)\n\nThe non-equidistant discrete Fourier transform (NDFT) evaluates the trigonometric polynomial with coefficients f_bmn at J nodes bmk_j in mathbbT^D, where mathbbT = -12 12) is the torus:\n\nhatf_j = sum_bmn in I_bmN f_bmn  mathrme^-2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j\nquad j = 1dotsJ qquad textit(equidistant to non-equidistant)\n\nIts adjoint maps values at the nodes back to the grid,\n\ny_bmn = sum_j=1^J hatf_j  mathrme^2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j\nquad bmn in I_bmN qquad textit(non-equidistant to equidistant)\n\nIn general the adjoint is not the inverse; see Density Compensation and Toeplitz Kernels.\n\nnote: Note\nThe indices in I_bmN are centred around zero, while an ordinary DFT runs over 0dotsN_d-1. An fftshift converts one representation into the other.","title":"NDFT"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Matrix-notation","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"With bmN = prod_d=1^D N_d the NDFT is the matrix–vector product hatbmf = bmA bmf with\n\nbmA = left( mathrme^-2pimathrmibmncdotbmk_j right)_j=1dotsJ bmn in I_bmN\nin mathbbC^J times bmN\n\nand the adjoint is bmy = bmA^mathsfH hatbmf. A plan in NFFT.jl behaves like this matrix: p * f, adjoint(p) * fHat, size(p) == (J, prod(N)).","title":"Matrix notation"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#NFFT","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"The direct evaluation costs mathcalO(J bmN) operations. The NFFT approximates bmA by a product of three matrices,\n\nbmA approx bmB bmF bmD\n\nwhich costs mathcalO(tildebmN log tildebmN + J W^D). Here tildebmN approx sigma bmN is the size of an oversampled grid with oversampling factor sigma  1, and W is the width of the window function in grid points. Each matrix is one stage of the algorithm:\n\nResampling correction bmD in mathbbC^tildebmN times bmN: divides bmf by the Fourier transform hatvarphi of the window function and places the result in the zero-padded oversampled array (including the fftshift).\nFFT bmF in mathbbC^tildebmN times tildebmN on the oversampled grid.\nResampling bmB in mathbbC^J times tildebmN: a sparse matrix that interpolates the oversampled grid at each node with the window function varphi, i.e. it combines the W^D grid points around the node.\n\nThe adjoint NFFT applies the adjoint stages in reverse order,\n\nbmA^mathsfH approx bmD^mathsfH bmF^mathsfH bmB^mathsfH\n\nThe first of them, bmB^mathsfH, adds the weighted value of each node to the grid points around it; this is the costly and hard-to-parallelise part of the adjoint.\n\nnote: Why resampling and not convolution\nThe matrix bmB is often called convolution and bmD deconvolution. Since bmB evaluates the window at node-dependent offsets, it is not shift-invariant and therefore not a convolution. We use the terms resampling and resampling correction, as in the paper.","title":"NFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Window,-width-and-oversampling","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"The accuracy of the approximation depends on three choices: the window function varphi, its width W and the oversampling factor sigma. A larger W or sigma reduces the error, at a higher cost in the resampling (W^D per node) or in the FFT (sigma^D). In the notation of the paper the window has the half width m, so W = 2m; NFFT.jl also allows odd widths.\n\nNFFT.jl chooses all three from the requested relative error, see Accuracy and Parameters.","title":"Window, width and oversampling"},{"category":"section","location":"background/#Real-valued-variants","page":"Mathematical Background","text":"The non-equidistant fast cosine and sine transforms (NFCT, NFST) of NFFT3 use real data and nodes in 0 12^D:\n\nhatf_j = sum_bmn f_bmn prod_d=1^D cos(2pi n_d k_dj)\nqquad\nhatf_j = sum_bmn f_bmn prod_d=1^D sin(2pi n_d k_dj)\n\nwith n_d = 0dotsN_d-1 (cosine) and n_d = 1dotsN_d (sine) for an array of size bmN. Their transposes take the role of the adjoint. NFFT.jl computes them as NFFTs of the even and odd extension of the data, see Cosine and Sine Transforms.","title":"Real-valued variants"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#Accuracy-and-Parameters","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"The NFFT approximates the NDFT; its error depends on the window function varphi, the window width W (in grid points) and the oversampling factor sigma (see Mathematical Background). NFFT.jl lets you state the error you need and chooses these three for you.","title":"Accuracy and Parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#From-reltol-to-parameters","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"reltol is the requested relative error in the ell^2 norm, for random data. For each combination of window family, W and sigma NFFT.jl carries a table with the optimised shape parameter of the window and the error it reaches. From all entries whose predicted error is below reltol, the plan takes the one with the lowest predicted run time. The cost model weighs the resampling (J W^D) against the FFT (sigma^D bmN log), so the choice depends on the dimension and on the ratio of nodes to grid size.\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nN = (48, 48)\nk = rand(2, 4000) .- 0.5\nf = randn(ComplexF64, N)\nexact = NDFTPlan(k, N) * f\nrelerr(p) = norm(p * f - exact) / norm(exact)\n\np = plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=1e-6)\nNFFTPlan{Float64} for an input of size (48, 48) along dims 1:2 at 4000 nodes: ExpSemicircle window, W = 7, σ = 2.0, predicted error 9.97192883345134e-7\n\nThe plan shows what it chose; NFFT.parameters returns the same as a named tuple:\n\nNFFT.parameters(p)\n(window = ExpSemicircle(), W = 7, σ = 2.0)\n\nOver a range of tolerances, the measured error stays below the requested one, and accuracy(p) predicts it well:\n\ntols = [10.0^-i for i in 2:14]\nplans = [plan_nfft(k, N; reltol=t) for t in tols]\nmeasured = relerr.(plans)\npredicted = accuracy.(plans)\n\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xscale=log10, yscale=log10, xreversed=true,\n          xlabel=\"requested reltol\", ylabel=\"relative error\",\n          xticks=LogTicks(-14:2:-2), yticks=LogTicks(-14:2:-2))\nlines!(ax, tols, tols; color=:gray60, linestyle=:dash, label=\"reltol\")\nscatterlines!(ax, tols, predicted; label=\"accuracy(p)\")\nscatterlines!(ax, tols, measured; label=\"measured\")\naxislegend(ax; position=:rt)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe chosen widths and oversampling factors:\n\n[(reltol=t, W=NFFT.parameters(q).W, σ=NFFT.parameters(q).σ) for (t, q) in zip(tols, plans)]\n13-element Vector{@NamedTuple{reltol::Float64, W::Int64, σ::Float64}}:\n (reltol = 0.010000000000000002, W = 4, σ = 1.25)\n (reltol = 0.001, W = 5, σ = 1.4)\n (reltol = 0.0001, W = 6, σ = 1.45)\n (reltol = 1.0e-5, W = 7, σ = 1.5)\n (reltol = 1.0e-6, W = 7, σ = 2.0)\n (reltol = 1.0000000000000001e-7, W = 8, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-8, W = 9, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-9, W = 10, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-10, W = 11, σ = 2.05)\n (reltol = 1.0e-11, W = 12, σ = 2.2)\n (reltol = 1.0000000000000002e-12, W = 13, σ = 2.2)\n (reltol = 1.0e-13, W = 14, σ = 2.2)\n (reltol = 1.0e-14, W = 15, σ = 2.5)\n\nBelow about 10^-14 rounding errors dominate in double precision; in single precision the floor is about 10^-6. A reltol below the floor gives the most accurate plan available.","title":"From reltol to parameters"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#Window-families","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"Two families have parameter tables:\n\nKaiserBessel: the continuous Kaiser–Bessel window,\nExpSemicircle: the \"exponential of semicircle\" window exp(beta((1-(2zW)^2)^e_0 - 1)).\n\nThe default window = :auto lets both compete. A fixed family restricts the search:\n\npkb = plan_nfft(k, N; window=KaiserBessel())\npes = plan_nfft(k, N; window=ExpSemicircle())\nNFFT.parameters(pkb), NFFT.parameters(pes)\n((window = KaiserBessel(), W = 10, σ = 1.75), (window = ExpSemicircle(), W = 9, σ = 2.05))\n\nHow the error falls with the width, at sigma = 2 and sigma = 125:\n\nWs = 4:14\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 380))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; yscale=log10, xlabel=\"window width W\", ylabel=\"relative error\",\n          xticks=Ws, yticks=LogTicks(-14:2:-2))\nfor (σ, style) in ((2.0, :solid), (1.25, :dash)), (win, name) in ((KaiserBessel(), \"Kaiser–Bessel\"),\n                                                                   (ExpSemicircle(), \"ES\"))\n  errs = [relerr(plan_nfft(k, N; window=win, W, σ)) for W in Ws]\n  scatterlines!(ax, Ws, errs; linestyle=style, label=\"$name, σ = $σ\")\nend\naxislegend(ax; position=:lb)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nA smaller sigma needs a wider window for the same error, but its FFT is cheaper; which is faster overall depends on D and on the number of nodes. This is the trade-off the automatic choice makes.","title":"Window families"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#Setting-the-parameters-yourself","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"W and σ restrict the search as well; with both given, only the window shape comes from the table. W may be odd. In the notation of the NFFT.jl paper the window has the half width m, so W = 2m.\n\np = plan_nfft(k, N; W=7, σ=1.5)\nNFFT.parameters(p), relerr(p)\n((window = ExpSemicircle(), W = 7, σ = 1.5), 6.13080993991677e-6)\n\nAny other window can be used through CustomWindow. The function receives the offset z in grid points and the width W; it must be symmetric and vanish for z ge W2. Without a table, W and σ must be given and accuracy(p) is unknown (NaN):\n\ngauss = CustomWindow((z, W) -> abs(z) < W / 2 ? exp(-(2z / W)^2 * 9) : 0.0)\npg = plan_nfft(k, N; window=gauss, W=8, σ=2.0)\nrelerr(pg), accuracy(pg)\n(6.343713256856872e-5, NaN)","title":"Setting the parameters yourself"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#FFT","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"The FFT on the oversampled grid uses FFTW. fft = FFTWBackend(flags=FFTW.MEASURE) lets FFTW measure its plans, which takes longer when creating the NFFT plan:\n\nusing FFTW\np = plan_nfft(k, N; fft=FFTWBackend(flags=FFTW.MEASURE))\n\nNFFT.jl computes only the parts of the oversampled FFT that are needed: the input is zero outside the bmN modes, and only those modes are kept in the adjoint.","title":"FFT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/accuracy/#All-options","page":"Accuracy and Parameters","text":"keyword meaning\nreltol requested relative error, default 1e-8\ndims dimensions to transform, see Transforms along Dimensions\neltype Float32 or Float64, default from the nodes\nnthreads number of threads, default Threads.nthreads()\nwindow :auto, KaiserBessel(), ExpSemicircle(), CustomWindow(φ)\nW, σ window width and oversampling factor\nfft FFT backend, default FFTWBackend()\ntilesize edge length of the tiles the nodes are sorted into (expert option)\n\nThe first four are part of the Abstract Interface and mean the same for every implementation; the others are specific to NFFT.jl. An unknown option is an error.\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"All options"},{"category":"section","location":"#NFFT.jl","page":"Home","text":"Fast Fourier transforms at non-equidistant nodes, in pure Julia.\n\nwarning: Development version\nThis documentation describes NFFT.jl 1.0, which is under development on the main branch and not released yet. Pkg.add(\"NFFT\") installs version 0.14, documented here. See Migrating from NFFT.jl 0.x.\n\nNFFT.jl computes the non-equidistant fast Fourier transform (NFFT, also called NUFFT, or gridding in MRI): a discrete Fourier transform where the nodes in one domain do not lie on a grid. It is accurate to a tolerance you choose and runs in mathcalO(bmN log bmN + J) operations for J nodes and an array of size bmN.\n\nusing NFFT\n\nk = rand(2, 10_000) .- 0.5           # 10 000 nodes in [-1/2, 1/2)²\nf = randn(ComplexF64, 256, 256)      # equidistant data\n\np = plan_nfft(k, size(f))            # relative error 1e-8 by default\nfHat = p * f                         # NFFT: equidistant → nodes\ng = adjoint(p) * fHat                # adjoint NFFT: nodes → equidistant","title":"NFFT.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#What-NFFT.jl-offers","page":"Home","text":"You ask for an accuracy, not for parameters. reltol is all you need to set. The plan picks the window function, its width and the oversampling factor that reach this error at the lowest predicted cost, and accuracy(p) tells you what it expects to achieve.\nFast. Tiled resampling with piecewise-polynomial window evaluation, an FFT that skips the zero padding, and multi-threading in every stage. See Performance for a comparison with FINUFFT and ducc.\nTransforms along some dimensions of an array (dims), with batch kernels that process many transforms with the same nodes at once, as needed for multi-coil MRI or OCT.\nReal data at the nodes (plan_rnfft, e.g. OCT), on a real grid.\nCosine and sine transforms (NFCT, NFST) for real data, with the definitions of NFFT3 and about the cost of a complex NFFT.\nSingle and double precision, with node positions always computed in double precision.\nThe three stages of the algorithm as an interface: resampling correction, FFT and resampling can be called one by one, for example to embed the NFFT into a Toeplitz approach or to profile it.\nOne interface for several implementations. AbstractNFFTs.jl defines plans, options and operations; NFFT.jl is one implementation, wrappers for FINUFFT and NFFT3 are others. Code written against the interface runs with each of them.","title":"What NFFT.jl offers"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"NFFT\")\n\nThis also installs AbstractNFFTs.jl, the interface package. Sampling density compensation and Toeplitz kernels are in NFFTTools.jl (Pkg.add(\"NFFTTools\")).","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Guide","page":"Home","text":"Mathematical Background defines the NDFT, the NFFT and its three stages.\nGetting Started shows plans, transforms, precision, threads and changing nodes.\nTransforms along Dimensions covers dims and batched transforms.\nAccuracy and Parameters explains how reltol becomes window, width and oversampling, and how to set these yourself.\nStages of the NFFT shows the stage interface.\nReal Data covers plan_rnfft for real values at the nodes (e.g. OCT).\nCosine and Sine Transforms covers the NFCT and NFST.\nDensity Compensation and Toeplitz Kernels shows NFFTTools.jl.\nPerformance compares NFFT.jl with other libraries.\nAbstract Interface describes AbstractNFFTs.jl and how to write an implementation.\nMigrating from NFFT.jl 0.x lists what changed in version 1.0.\nAPI lists all exported functions and types.","title":"Guide"},{"category":"section","location":"#Citing","page":"Home","text":"The source code is licensed under the MIT license. If you use NFFT.jl in your work, please cite\n\nT. Knopp, M. Boberg and M. Grosser, NFFT.jl: Generic and Fast Julia Implementation of the Nonequidistant Fast Fourier Transform, SIAM Journal on Scientific Computing 45(3), C179–C205, 2023 (arXiv:2208.00049).\n\nA BibTeX entry is in NFFT.bib in the root of the repository.","title":"Citing"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contact","page":"Home","text":"Bugs and ideas go to the issue tracker, questions to the discussions.","title":"Contact"},{"category":"section","location":"#Contributors","page":"Home","text":"Tobias Knopp\nRobert Dahl Jacobsen\nMirco Grosser\nJakob Assländer\nMosè Giordano\n\nThe complete list is on GitHub.","title":"Contributors"},{"category":"section","location":"migration/#Migrating-from-NFFT.jl-0.x","page":"Migrating from NFFT.jl 0.x","text":"NFFT.jl 1.0 is a new implementation with a cleaner interface. Most code that only uses plan_nfft, *, mul!, adjoint and reltol runs unchanged. The changes:\n\nNFFT.jl 0.x NFFT.jl 1.0\nm, σ W = 2m (window width; odd widths allowed), σ\nwindow = :kaiser_bessel, … window = KaiserBessel(), ExpSemicircle(), CustomWindow(φ); default :auto\nprecompute = POLYNOMIAL / TENSOR / LINEAR / FULL removed; the window is always evaluated as a piecewise polynomial\nblocking, sortNodes, blockSize removed (always tiled); tilesize as an expert option\nstoreDeconvolutionIdx, LUTSize removed\nfftflags = FFTW.MEASURE fft = FFTWBackend(flags=FFTW.MEASURE)\ntiming keyword, TimingStats removed; time the stages instead\npositional array type plan_nfft(Array, k, N) storage = Array\nNFFT._use_threads[] = false nthreads = 1\nconvolve!, convolve_transpose! resampling!, resampling_adjoint!\ndeconvolve!, deconvolve_transpose! resampling_correction!, resampling_correction_adjoint!\np.Ñ oversampled_size(p)\n\nBehaviour that changed:\n\nUnknown options are errors. In 0.x, misspelled or unsupported keywords could be ignored silently.\nreltol is met. The plan chooses window, width and oversampling from tables of measured errors; accuracy(p) reports the predicted error.\nNodes are checked. Nodes outside -12 12 or not finite throw an ArgumentError.\ndims accepts contiguous ranges of dimensions, not only a single dimension, and uses batch kernels.\nGPU plans are not yet available in 1.0.","title":"Migrating from NFFT.jl 0.x"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Stages-of-the-NFFT","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"The NFFT consists of three stages, resampling correction, FFT and resampling (see Mathematical Background). The stage interface exposes them one by one on the oversampled array of size oversampled_size(p). This is useful for methods that operate on the oversampled grid, such as Toeplitz embeddings, for teaching, and for profiling.\n\nusing NFFT, LinearAlgebra, Random, CairoMakie\nRandom.seed!(1)\n\nN = (256, 256)\nk = rand(2, 100_000) .- 0.5\np = plan_nfft(k, N)\nÑ = oversampled_size(p)\n(432, 432)","title":"Stages of the NFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Direct-NFFT","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"f = randn(ComplexF64, N)\ng = zeros(ComplexF64, Ñ)\nfHat = zeros(ComplexF64, size(k, 2))\n\nresampling_correction!(p, f, g)   # D: divide by φ̂, zero-pad\noversampled_fft!(p, g)            # F\nresampling!(p, g, fHat)           # B: evaluate at the nodes\nfHat ≈ p * f\ntrue","title":"Direct NFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Adjoint-NFFT","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"The adjoint runs the adjoint stages in reverse order:\n\nback = zeros(ComplexF64, N)\nresampling_adjoint!(p, fHat, g)           # Bᴴ: add node values to the grid\noversampled_bfft!(p, g)                   # Fᴴ\nresampling_correction_adjoint!(p, g, back) # Dᴴ: crop, divide by φ̂\nback ≈ adjoint(p) * fHat\ntrue","title":"Adjoint NFFT"},{"category":"section","location":"generated/examples/stages/#Where-the-time-goes","page":"Stages of the NFFT","text":"Timing the stages shows which part dominates. The FFT stages of this interface transform the full oversampled array, while p * f skips the zero padding, so the sum is a bit larger than a complete transform.\n\ntmin(op) = (op(); minimum(@elapsed(op()) for _ in 1:5))\nt = [tmin(() -> resampling_correction!(p, f, g)),\n     tmin(() -> oversampled_fft!(p, g)),\n     tmin(() -> resampling!(p, g, fHat)),\n     tmin(() -> resampling_adjoint!(p, fHat, g)),\n     tmin(() -> oversampled_bfft!(p, g)),\n     tmin(() -> resampling_correction_adjoint!(p, g, back))]\n\nfig = Figure(size=(640, 300))\nax = Axis(fig[1, 1]; xlabel=\"time / ms\", yticks=(1:2, [\"NFFT\", \"adjoint\"]))\ncolors = Makie.wong_colors()\nnames = [\"resampling correction\", \"FFT\", \"resampling\"]\nfor (row, idx) in ((1, 1:3), (2, [6, 5, 4]))\n  left = 0.0\n  for (c, i) in enumerate(idx)\n    barplot!(ax, [row], [1e3t[i]]; direction=:x, offset=left, fillto=left, color=colors[c])\n    left += 1e3t[i]\n  end\nend\nLegend(fig[2, 1], [PolyElement(color=colors[c]) for c in 1:3], names;\n       orientation=:horizontal, framevisible=false)\nfig\nFigure()\n\nThe stage interface needs a plan without batch dimensions (dims covering all dimensions).\n\n\n\nThis page was generated using Literate.jl.","title":"Where the time goes"}]
}
